# Shell scripts stay LF, always.
#
# core.autocrlf is on for this repo, so a .sh checked out on Windows gets CRLF.
# That is harmless until the same file runs on the Linux container, where the
# trailing \r becomes part of the interpreter path and the script dies with
# "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M: no such file or directory". One line here is
# cheaper than diagnosing that during a deploy.
*.sh text eol=lf
